{{Politics of Okaiken}}

The '''Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare''' ({{Nihongo|厚生労働省|Kōsei-rōdō-shō}}, commonly abbreviated '''MHLW''' or '''Kōrō-shō''') is a cabinet-level ministry of the [[Government of Okaiken]] responsible for public healthcare, epidemic prevention, occupational safety, labor standards enforcement, employment welfare, social security pensions, and child-care administration. With an annual expenditure budget of approximately 480 billion Sen—commanding roughly 30 percent of total national spending—and an administrative and hospital personnel establishment of 312,500 employees, the ministry is the largest department in the state by fiscal footprint and direct civic interface.

The ministry oversees the [[Okaiken National Health Service]] (ONHS), established under the [[National Universal Health Act]] of 2023, transitioning the nation to a single-payer tax-funded healthcare system free at the point of delivery. The incumbent Minister of Health, Labour and Welfare is [[Satoshi Kato]] of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP), who entered office on 20 May 2026 within the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]]. In coalition governance, Kato works in close institutional alignment with Deputy Prime Minister [[Rento Nakayama]], who coordinates broad social policy in his capacity as Minister of Social Affairs.

==Historical development and bureaucratic consolidation==

===Predecessor departments to the 2001 unification===

Prior to the structural consolidation of the central bureaucracy in 2001, healthcare and industrial labor were directed by two separate cabinet departments. The Ministry of Health and Welfare (厚生省, ''Kōsei-shō'') was inaugurated in 1938 to direct public sanitation, military medical mobilization, and national health insurance cooperatives. Following the post-war reconstruction, the Ministry of Labour (労働省, ''Rōdō-shō'') was established in 1947 to guarantee collective bargaining rights, enforce the Labor Standards Act, and supervise occupational safety during the post-war industrial miracle.

Throughout the late twentieth century, the two departments operated under distinct institutional philosophies. Health and Welfare focused on actuarial stability across the National Pension system and healthcare cost containment, whereas Labour negotiated industrial wage settlements and arbitrated disputes between union federations and corporate conglomerates. The 2001 Central Government Reform merged both departments into the modern MHLW, seeking an integrated administrative response to the compounding structural crises of national demographic aging, declining fertility rates, and the gradual erosion of lifetime corporate employment.

===Universal healthcare rollout and modern labor reforms===

In December 2023, the National Diet enacted the landmark [[National Universal Health Act]], dissolving fragmented employment insurance societies in favor of the consolidated Okaiken National Health Service (ONHS). Under the guidance of former Health Minister Rento Nakayama and his successor Satoshi Kato, the ministry oversaw the multi-year transition ensuring universal clinical access without co-payments, financing the system through progressive income taxation and central treasury transfers.

Simultaneously, the ministry confronted acute labor deregulations stemming from the rise of platform subcontracting and non-regular employment. Following the March 2026 Nakayama Station railway fatality, MHLW labor inspectors uncovered systematic labor exploitation and falsified shift logs among private railway maintenance contractors. Under Minister Kato, the ministry expanded the authority of regional Labor Standards Inspection Offices (労働基準監督署, ''Rōki-sho''), conducting unannounced forensic audits into subcontractor staffing levels and cracking down on excessive overtime violations across transport, logistics, and healthcare networks.

